AB  - Fast and accurate current control is a fundamental requirement for grid-connected voltage source inverters, as the performance of the inner current loop directly affects the stability, bandwidth, and power quality of higher-level control functions. While deep reinforcement learning (DRL) has recently been explored for power inverter control, many existing studies rely on training and evaluation in the same environment, which limits the assessment of generalization and deployment feasibility. This paper proposes a deployment-oriented DRL framework for inverter current control that enables efficient training and robust cross-model implementation. Instead of training on computationally intensive switching-level simulations, the control policy is learned using a discrete state-space model of the inverter dynamics in Python. To improve robustness against modeling inaccuracies, parameter randomization is introduced during training to account for uncertainty in filter inductance. The learned policy is subsequently deployed without retraining in a high-fidelity switching inverter model implemented on an OPAL-RT real-time simulation platform. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed controller achieves accurate current tracking, reduced harmonic distortion, and strong robustness to parameter variations when transferred from the training model to the real-time switching inverter environment. These results highlight the potential of combining efficient state-space training with robust policy design for practical learning-based control of power inverters.
