AB  - Space exploration is a captivating yet challenging endeavor. During the exploration, space probes face several power-related issues. The power of solar arrays and DC voltage fluctuate greatly. There are also discretized electric propulsion (EP) loads and inevitable energy conversion losses. To overcome these challenges, an energy management strategy named EEF-MPC is present in present work, which means efficiency estimating and a fuzzy-model predictive control. Firstly, the efficiency of the power conversion module (PCM) is often uncertain. To deal with this problem, a power compensation strategy is introduced, which is based on a disturbance observer that estimates the efficiency real-time. It can compensate for the losses caused by the uncertain efficiency. Secondly, fuzzy rules are used to analyze the state of health (SOH) of the battery. Based on the SOH, the charge and discharge modes of battery can be controlled, as well as the EP levels is adjusted accordingly to optimize energy usage. Then, a discrete state-space model for the battery is established in the form of control increments. Through quadratic programming, the compensated battery current can be tracked and controlled accurately. Simulation results indicate that the EEF-MPC strategy has a maximum efficiency estimation deviation of approximately 4 %. Additionally, the maximum error of current is reduced by about 35 % compared with the traditional MPC method, which demonstrates that the EEF-MPC strategy can manage energy effectively in complex conditions and improve battery current tracking performance. Finally, a hardware-in-loop experiment is conducted, validating the effectiveness of the proposed strategy.
