AB  - Proactive water-thermal management is essential for securing operational safety and extending the lifespan of proton exchange membrane (PEM) electrolyzer cells under dynamic renewable power fluctuations. In this research, a predictive water and thermal management strategy based on model predictive control (MPC) is developed and experimentally validated. A fully coupled transient mechanism model is established to capture multiphysics dynamics over a wide operational range up to 4 A/cm2. To meet real time computational constraints, the nonlinear governing equations are analytically reconstructed into a discrete state-space predictor via localized linearization, preserving physical consistency. This specific control formulation aims to proactively optimize convective cooling and achieve electro-thermal stabilization. Experimental investigations under unregulated constant flow operations highlight severe thermal risks driven by internal heat accumulation. In contrast, dynamic control validations demonstrate that the proposed predictive strategy effectively suppresses the temperature rise and keeps the cell temperature near the target threshold of 62.0 °C under both step-current transitions and random renewable power fluctuations. In addition, this approach effectively suppresses transient voltage overshoots, improving electro-thermal stability under the tested dynamic operating conditions.
